{
  "dom_id": "step-q324056",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/6f3382db4b66df920c22c7050ea5e22c56892094c51d8889872b7bfcb541f684.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{2}.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/ff58f07acb93044b6bca56ca30fe5f3b8447603ca9cb6d120559d287c435b987.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\text{\u00a0}{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\text{\u00a0}\\text{and}\\text{\u00a0}b=2\\pi .$",
